WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.768
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.235
We're here with Tom Holland.

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:04.871
Last time you were here,
right before the holidays,

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:07.373
we were talking about
you had not had time

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:08.541
and, obviously,
you're a busy guy,

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:10.510
to finish your Christmas
shopping for your family.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.877
I know you're very close
to your family.

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:13.812
You always have at least
one brother with you

00:00:13.812 --> 00:00:15.782
when you come here.
It's very nice to see him.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:18.918
Did you get it done? Did you get
your Christmas shopping done?

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:19.652
-No.
-Okay.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:20.886
[ Laughter ]

00:00:20.886 --> 00:00:22.655
-Thank you for bringing that up.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:24.290
I just honestly,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.393
I'd finished the "Spider-Man"
press tour and I was so busy

00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:30.797
and I was so tired,
that I just didn't do it.

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:31.931
[ Laughter ]

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.265
And that's terrible.
That's terrible.

00:00:33.265 --> 00:00:35.468
I've told my family and they're
already in agreement

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.570
that next year will be
a double gift year.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.805
-Oh, wow! So they're
basically -- Yeah, okay.

00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:42.608
-Yeah, put your orders in now
and I'll sort you out.

00:00:42.608 --> 00:00:43.609
Don't worry.
-So they're ultimately

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:45.911
patient with you.

00:00:45.911 --> 00:00:47.279
They're not
too judgmental of it.

00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:49.782
-No, of course not.
I mean, my family are wonderful

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:52.251
and they know how hard I work
and the traveling

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:53.819
and, you know, like, you know,

00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:55.121
the press tours are tough.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.123
It looks really glamorous
and it is very glamorous --

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.191
I'm not complaining,
but I am complaining.

00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:02.228
[ Laughter ]
Because it's really tiring.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.263
-That's the part that's the most
remarkable to me.

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:06.866
We always talk backstage
because people don't understand

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:09.235
that it's almost exactly as long
as it takes to shoot a movie,

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:12.438
especially a movie the size
of movies that you're making,

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.407
you have to go out and then talk
about them forever.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.076
-Yeah. And, honestly,
I'm so sick of my own voice.

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:20.647
-Yeah.
-It does get to that stage.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:21.881
We'd finished work the other day

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.550
and I bumped into someone
in a bar

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.152
and they asked me
about "Uncharted"

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:28.120
and, immediately, I just went
into press mode,

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.222
like "It's a movie
that ticks all the boxes.

00:01:30.222 --> 00:01:32.024
It's funny. It's exciting."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.393
And I was like,
"Stop talking like that!"

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:36.395
-And then, at the bar, did the
manager come over and go,

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:37.430
"Back to work, Holland!"?
-Yeah.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.932
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:41.367
-Now, "Uncharted" --
this movie came about

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.804
because you wanted to play
sort of a young James Bond.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.539
Is that right?
-Yes. Yeah.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.707
It didn't work out quite
as planned.

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:50.510
[ Laughter ]
-But this is that kind
of character.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.011
-No, essentially, I just --

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.946
I'm such a big fan
of James Bond

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:55.781
and I, you know, I've loved
Daniel Craig's movies

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:57.717
and I just thought,

00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:00.486
if they were to move on,
it would be really exciting

00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:03.089
to see like how James Bond
becomes James Bond.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:04.957
So I pitched this idea
for a movie,

00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:07.526
which is kind of a stupid idea
where, essentially,

00:02:07.526 --> 00:02:08.728
you wouldn't tell the audience

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:10.763
that it was a James Bond movie
until the end,

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:13.333
but from a marketing point
of view, that's a nightmare.

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:15.668
[ Laughter ]
Like that just doesn't make
any sense.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.637
-And you would never be able
to keep that secret.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.939
-No, mate.
If I got cast as Bond,

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.808
I'd be like,
"I am James Bond," tweet.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:24.043
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.945
But, no, Tom Aspinall
really liked the idea

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.513
and, you know, they've been
trying to crack the case

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:29.015
with "Uncharted"
for a long time.

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.283
You know, there's been loads
of different actors.

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.220
Mark Wahlberg, at one point,
was going to play Nate

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.687
and now he's old,

00:02:35.687 --> 00:02:37.557
[ Applause ]
so he plays Sully.

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:40.126
-Yeah. This is sort of like
breaking Hollywood news,

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:41.961
that Mark Wahlberg is old now.

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:42.995
Is that what you're saying?
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:44.263
[ Laughter ]
No, I've been saying that

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:46.632
on all the press tours.

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:47.566
It's been really fun.

00:02:47.566 --> 00:02:49.435
-It seems like, yeah.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.304
Mark Wahlberg seems like
a person that you would,

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:55.274
as a costar, that you would be
able to tease relentlessly

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:56.709
back and forth
because I would imagine

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.444
he gives it
as well as he takes it.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:01.413
-Yeah.
You can tease Mark,

00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:04.183
but he's also quite a scary guy.
-Yes, he is.

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:06.286
[ Laughter ]
-So like I have a security guard

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:07.252
who's one of my best mates,

00:03:07.252 --> 00:03:08.521
but I only tease Mark

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.389
when Jack's around.

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:11.023
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:13.292
I'll be like --

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:15.028
No, I'm not going to say
what I was going to say.

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.228
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.228 --> 00:03:18.231
-Yeah. [Indistinct] right now
if I was like,

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:20.366
"Well, Jack's not here,
but Mark is."

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:21.534
I'd be like, "Jack!"

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:25.071
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:26.906
-We've talked in the past
as well about, you know,

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:30.809
this is another big film,
great action.

00:03:30.809 --> 00:03:32.478
There's a stunt in this film
that I want to ask you about

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.179
because, obviously,
you're no stranger to stunts,

00:03:34.179 --> 00:03:36.415
but I've never seen somebody --

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:39.685
You are in mid-air
and you get hit by a car.

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:40.987
-That's true.
-[Both] Yeah.

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:44.724
-Now, how do they bring that
to you as an idea

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:46.826
and then you just call
your stunt man right away

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:48.227
and say, "Bad news"?

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:51.029
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.029 --> 00:03:54.033
-Yeah, I dunno.
It's one of those things where

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:58.171
this, for me,
as a young kid growing up,

00:03:58.171 --> 00:03:59.705
the Indiana Joneses
of the world

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:02.474
was my favorite genre of film.
-The best.

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:05.144
-So, to do it myself
was really exciting,

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:08.581
but then, I had to do it
and I broke myself.

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.816
Like they tell you, "Right,
we're going to do this stunt

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:13.052
where you're going to jump
into the back of an airplane

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:15.621
and then you get hit
by a car out of the airplane."

00:04:15.621 --> 00:04:17.757
And you're like, "Wow!
That sounds amazing!"

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.360
And then you shoot it
and you're on take 12

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.360
and like, "Can we go again?"

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.763
And you're like, "No!
I'm done!"

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.197
[ Laughter ]
But it was tough.

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:27.266
I was actually on set
on "Spider-Man,"

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:28.901
pitching the movie to Zendaya.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.937
I was going through the film
scene-by-scene

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.205
and I told her that.
I was like,

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:34.073
"Oh, there's this great bit
where I get by a car

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.643
out of a plane and she's like,
"I have to stop you there.

00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:39.211
What on Earth
is this movie about?"

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:41.313
[ Laughter ]
But it was fun.
It was a lot of fun

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:43.750
and the stunts is something
I've always really enjoyed.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:45.318
I have two amazing stunt doubles

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:47.786
who I've worked with
for a number of years, now,

00:04:47.786 --> 00:04:50.923
and we have a great little thing
going on where, you know,

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.092
our motto is that things
should look dangerous

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:55.994
while being safe.
That's not always the case.

00:04:55.994 --> 00:04:57.596
You know, there's bangs
and bruises here and there,

00:04:57.596 --> 00:04:58.864
but it's part of the job.

00:04:58.864 --> 00:05:00.933
But, yeah,
being hit by the car --

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.635
My mum actually came to set
that day.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:05.470
-Oh, wow. Did she know it was
Hit by the Car Day?

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:06.538
-No!
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.538 --> 00:05:07.940
-And then, the next day,

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.242
after hit by the car,
was Drown in the Well Day.

00:05:10.242 --> 00:05:11.476
-Wow!
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.476 --> 00:05:12.644
-Mate, it was brutal.

00:05:12.644 --> 00:05:14.046
-Yeah. I guess that would've
been awful,

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:15.514
if she like read the script
and said,

00:05:15.514 --> 00:05:18.417
"I want to be there on the day
you get hit by a car

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:19.551
and I definitely want
to be there

00:05:19.551 --> 00:05:20.686
on drown in the well.

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.521
-It's that thing for me like

00:05:22.521 --> 00:05:24.490
whenever my mum and dad come
to visit set

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.959
is always the day
where I don't work.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:28.661
It's always the day where I'm
in my trailer

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.562
and I have nothing to do
because they're like,

00:05:30.562 --> 00:05:32.130
"There's a problem
with the sound stage,"

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.599
or they're
doing a different scene.

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:34.967
You're just,
"We need to wait for you."

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:36.669
So my parents were always like,
"Why are you so tired

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.070
all the time?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.505
Like what is wrong
with our son?"

00:05:40.505 --> 00:05:42.642
And then, obviously,
coming to set on those two days

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:45.277
consecutively, I was like,
"This is why I'm so tired

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:46.512
all the time."
-Yeah. You're like, you know,

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.180
"You get hit by a car
out of a plane

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.915
and buy Christmas gifts
for the whole family."

00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:51.984
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:55.588
-Hey, man, it is always a full
pleasure to have you here.

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:57.056
-Thank you.
-Keep up the great work.
Thank you so much

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:58.257
for making time for us, Tom.
-It was a pleasure.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:05:59.592
-You guys, that's Tom Holland.

